[{:seon.task/name "avet-seek-allocates"
  :seon.task/plan "One lookup of an entity by a unique attribute (seon.task/eid: (d/datoms db :avet a v), the same path as datahike.db.utils/entid) costs 13-17 us and 23-32 KB at 100-10k tasks, growing with log n. The work is one seek through 2-3 B-tree nodes with ~6 comparisons each and one datom answered: ~3 us and ~1.3 KB. Find where the seek allocates in our Datahike fork (reference-code/datahike: datahike.db.utils, datahike.index) and our persistent-sorted-set port (reference-code/persistent-sorted-set, E4a) on Jolt, and fix the cause there (profile with seon.profile / seon.cost/measure). Done when seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek is green with its :overrun key deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-small-transaction-costs-its-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-21: d/with of one 5-datom definition record costs 221-233 us and 148-162 KB, flat in program size (100-10k vars). Estimate from the work: ~15 index inserts at ~1.3 us (seon.cost/units :sorted/assoc) = 20-30 us. ~10x: find Datahike's per-transaction overhead on Jolt (tx report, schema checks, index wrappers) with seon.profile, and fix it in our fork."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/a-definition-record-costs-its-datoms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-store-opens-from-any-path"
  :seon.task/plan "A store's identity is (hasch/uuid path) (seon.db/store-cfg, db.clj:64) and Datahike also keeps the original path inside the stored config, so a copied or moved store does not open (store-identity-mismatch), and a branch's config taken from (:config @conn) points back at the original path (seen 2026-10-21 copying t5/out/real3.store). Runs are kept records: they must open wherever they are copied or archived. Record the identity once in the store at creation and read it back on open; never derive it from where the store happens to be."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-copied-store-opens-at-its-new-path}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-its-attributes"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by lane STORES, 2026-10-21 (why a fresh store's open was ~200 ms): the one schema transaction (seon.db/schema, 129 attributes, 407 datoms) costs 45-49 ms even in memory (d/db-with on an empty db; half the schema 15.5-16.7 ms: superlinear), against 0.12 ms for a 2-datom note. Cause: reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:148 update-rschema recomputes the whole rschema (dbu/rschema (:schema db)) after EVERY schema datom (called at :480, :491, :521, :575, :596), so a schema tx of n attributes costs O(n * attributes). Correct: the rschema is derived once per transaction, after its last schema datom (or updated by the one attribute that changed), on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike fork. Estimate: ~407 datom inserts at ~1.3 us plus one rschema derivation over 129 attributes (~50 us): about 1 ms. Since branches-as-in-production the schema tx runs once per store (a check run, the test store), not per boot, so this is no longer on any boot path; it still prices every store's creation and any schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-schema-transaction-is-linear-in-its-attributes}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by lane STORES, 2026-10-21 (asked by lane RUNNER: can parallel check steps be several roots on one store?). Writing different branches from several processes is safe and is already the design: each agent process commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: d/branch! (datahike.versioning/branch!) appends the :branches log with konserve.core/append (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three separate writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only; konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token (lmdb.jolt:28) because LMDB serializes single transactions, not this read-modify-write. So two processes creating branches at once can each link their element after the same last one and one branch drops out of d/branches (its head key stays, so seon.db/branch? still finds it), and a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. The entity-id counter (datahike.writing/eid-allocators) and root's agent leases (seon.db/lease-base, read once at boot) are per process too: two roots mint overlapping ids on different mains (harmless until those mains merge, which they never do). Production has one root per store, the only process that creates branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch), so it never meets this; several roots on one store at once (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/root beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) do. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or the rule 'one root per store at a time' is stated in DRAGONS with its edge (seon.db/open! refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks should share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-mints-in-a-block-of-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by lane STORES, 2026-10-21, building the lease ceiling (seon.fences/leases-and-store-size-are-declared-bounds): bin/check scale went red when an agent's merge-main! brought in main's entities minted in later agents' blocks and its next id followed them past its own ceiling (reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc next-eid minted (inc (max issued (:max-eid db)))). Fixed for agents on jolt/eid-lease: a process with a ceiling mints from its own counter alone. Root has no ceiling, so it still mints at (inc (max issued (:max-eid main))): after root merges agent n's branch, main's max-eid lies inside agent n's block and root's next entity (a recorded error, an attempt, a boot fact) takes an id agent n may mint next on its live branch; when that branch merges, two entities share one id. Root's own block is [roster max at boot, lease-base) (seon.db/lease-base: agents' blocks start past it). Correct: root takes that block with seon.db/lease! at boot (a ceiling at lease-base), and when it is spent takes the next free block through the same rule agents use, rather than a refusal (root's writes must not stop); the proof merges an agent's branch into main, has root write, and asserts root's new id lies in root's block."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/root-mints-in-its-own-block-after-a-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-store-gains-new-attributes-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root 2026-10-21 restarting the standing root after CONFIG landed: `seon.db/open!` transacted `seon.db/schema` only when it seeded a new store, so a kept store (runs are kept) never gained an attribute added later. Root's boot fact (`seon.task/boot-tx` with `seon.config/facts`) was then refused (`:seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes` not in the store's schema), `seon.root/start!` ignored the refusal, and the root ran with no boot fact: `seon.task/root` answered nil, the door's `agents` op failed in `seon.task/entity-value`'s contract, and no task could start. Fix at the owner: `open!` transacts the attributes the store lacks (`seon.db/install-missing!`, one transaction, nothing when nothing is missing; Datahike accepts new attribute definitions at any time), and `start!` ends the boot on a refused boot fact instead of ignoring the answer. Not covered: an attribute whose definition changed (type, cardinality, uniqueness); that is a migration, filed when one is first needed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/opening-a-kept-store-installs-the-attributes-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-opens-where-it-lies"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by lane NEXTGEN (2026-10-21) reading a kept store for `seon.root/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote`: `seon.db/store-cfg` names a store by `(hasch/uuid path)` of the path given, and Datahike's connect compares it with the id stored at creation (`datahike.connector`, :store-identity-mismatch), so a store renamed or copied is refused at its new path: an APFS clone of next/runs/store-full-20261021 (renamed from next/runs/store) answered `Store identity mismatch: connecting to wrong database` with :stored-id 060b3c6f-... (the old path's) against :connect-id 1de1d81b-... A kept run is a record (owner): it must open wherever it is kept. Correct: the store's identity is read from the store itself (its stored config) when it exists, minted from the path only at creation; bin/worktree's note (a cloned target/runs/store fails) then no longer holds. Replaces the path-derived id for existing stores."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-22 on the standing root's store (next/runs/store): data.mdb 1,305 MB, up from 747 MB at 2026-10-21 21:56 and 801 MB at 22:15; 278 branches under 68 run mains (every root boot and every check run on this store starts a new main `run-<time>-<id>` beside the old ones; every attempt's branch is kept). Root raised `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` from 1 GiB to 64 GiB on 2026-10-21 after two agents died on Datahike's `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` while recording, without confirming the cause was MDB_MAP_FULL: that hid the growth instead of addressing it (owner: \"What are we covering up and not addressing?\"). Earlier evidence: a kept branch measured 8.0 MB for 1,020 datoms because each per-eval commit rewrites ~18 index nodes (DECISIONS, \"Storage reclaim\"; owner ruling: GC keeps only what live branch heads reach, then compaction, as an :io proc when a measured dead/live ratio passes a dial, or on demand). Correct, in order: (1) measure what fills the store: bytes per branch and per commit, the share unreachable from any branch head, the bytes a run main and its branches hold; (2) state the expected cost of a commit from what it writes (its datoms and the index nodes they touch) and make an overrun a failing cost proof; (3) reclaim: the GC and compaction the ruling names, through Datahike's and konserve's own mechanisms (reference-code/datahike online-gc, konserve), never by deleting a branch; which run mains and branches are kept is the owner's call, asked with the measurement; (4) then set the map from the measured bound, with that provenance. A failing commit's cause is now recorded (task seon.agent/a-death-names-its-cause): confirm whether the two deaths were MDB_MAP_FULL. Measured 2026-10-22 (lane LIGHT, test/seon/bench_commit.clj on fresh LMDB stores, bf 64, seeds of 5k/45k/225k datoms): `d/branch!` writes 3 keys, 382 bytes (light); a 1-datom commit 11-14 keys, 16.8-27.7 KB (7-10 index nodes of 1.7-3.6 KB plus the commit and graph records); one eval record (5 datoms) 11-16 keys, 18.6-33.8 KB; 100 evals committed one by one 4.9-6.9 MB with 200-byte printed values and 13.7-19.3 MB with 2 KB ones (49-193 KB per commit), against 157-546 KB for the same 100 in one commit (26-35x). Real turns are worse: `seon.turn/act!` records the context the model read (`:seon.turn/context`), its reply and reasoning, so 50 turns with a 32 KB context committed per turn wrote 62 MB for 1.9 MB of their own values (32x; 8.9 MB at 10 turns per commit, 4.1 MB in one). Root's estimate (each commit rewrites the root-to-leaf paths, ~50 KB, 100 commits ~5 MB) holds for small values; the cause it misses dominates: Datahike holds every value inline in its index leaves (each twice, :eavt and :aevt), appends always land in the tail leaf, and a copy-on-write commit rewrites that whole leaf, so a commit costs about bf/2 x the size of the values already in the tail leaf (leaves of up to 1.3 MB were rewritten per turn), not its own size. Branching factor is not the fix (bf 16: 1.5x less). The run-ahead writer (seon.agent/transact!, commit at idle or after the flush window) already commits about once per turn, the cadence the owner ruled. Fix at the owner: hold values over a threshold out of the index leaves, written once as their own content-addressed key with the datom carrying the reference (Postgres TOAST; v1's seon.blob/inline-or-stage, 4,096 bytes), in our Datahike fork's node codec so every reader sees plain values: emulated, 50 per-turn commits then write ~2.0 MB of index plus each value once (~4 MB at 32 KB contexts, 15x less; 9x at 8 KB). A dependency and format change: root's go first. The proof `seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes` is red: 20 per-turn commits of an 8 KB-context turn wrote 3.84 MB against a bound of 1.07 MB (the one commit, 410 KB, plus 32 KB of path per commit). Owner, 2026-10-22: store each turn's basis, not its context text (lane BASIS, jolt/basis): a turn records `:seon.turn/basis` (the commit it read, task, attempt, turn, the proofs' results, red assertions, refusals and run errors, whether unmerged entries show, the last evaluations' transcript while no earlier turn shows them; ~350 bytes) and `:seon.turn/evals` (its own evaluations as the next turns show them), and `seon.context/render` makes the exact text from it on that commit; the turn loop sends that same render. Re-measured with test/seon/bench_commit.clj (seed 2k, bf 64, 50 turns, a 2 KB eval record, 1.5 KB reply, 3 KB reasoning, 2 KB evals): per-turn commits wrote 24.4 MB (488 KB a turn) at an 8 KB context and 61.3 MB (1.23 MB a turn) at 32 KB before, 16.0 MB (321 KB a turn) after at either size (1.5x and 3.8x less); 10 turns a commit 3.5/8.8 MB -> 2.3 MB; one commit 1.6 MB -> 1.0 MB. The proof is still red, 3.84 MB -> 1.63 MB against a bound of 0.84 MB (1.9x over): the remaining gap is the values still held inline in the tail leaves (the reply, reasoning, evals and the eval record's printed value), the out-of-line fix above. A past turn's text needs its commit: a GC that keeps only branch heads must keep the commits turns name, or past contexts become unreadable."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes}}]
